"A system context diagram explains one software system through its users and directly connected external systems.\n\nUse when: Introduce system responsibilities to a mixed audience. Agree what the team owns before decomposing implementation.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need deployment instances or internal modules.\n\nVisual grammar: One focal system has a clear name and responsibility. Surrounding nodes represent user roles and external systems. Verb-labeled arrows describe interactions across the focal boundary.\n\nBuild: State the focal system and the question the view answers. Identify user roles and direct external dependencies from evidence. Place users and dependencies around the focal system without implying organizational rank. Use short interaction labels and detour arrows around text with generous margins.\n\nCheck: Every supporting element directly relates to the focal system. The boundary reflects responsibility rather than network location. Internal databases and components have been deferred to a deeper view. Unknown or planned integrations use explicit labels, with no ambiguous crossing edges.\n\nPitfalls: Using vendor logos without explaining system responsibilities. Drawing two systems as the focal subject and obscuring scope.\n\nExample: A booking service sits between Travelers, a Payment Provider, and an Airline Reservation System.\n\nAlternatives: System landscape diagram, Container diagram."
